 "note": "The castle fortification registry, extracted verbatim from FantasyWorldGenerator b67c99b after castle_planner.load_castles() accepted it. `registry` is the complete source document; nothing was filtered out and nothing may be. castle_planner.plan_castle reads `modules` and `join_rules` and threads both into castle_geometry.build_wall_network, which validates end-type compatibility and the thickness and height deltas along a curtain; `kits` drives ring, court, landmark and service placement and `kit.half_m` sets the plan footprint before neighbour clamping. fill_castles writes world['castle_plans'], called from terrain_history at stage 16 and again on an age advance. THE `kits` LIST ORDER IS AN RNG CONTRACT. select_kit does random.Random(sha256(f'{seed}:castle-kit:{fortress_id}').hexdigest()).choices(kits, weights=[max(1, int(k.get('weight', 1))) for k in kits], k=1), and random.choices builds cumulative weights and bisects a single uniform draw, so swapping two kits of EQUAL weight still hands a different kit to the same fortress. Append, never insert. `modules` is keyed by name and read by name, and the ids stored into a plan are strings ('building_id': structure_id), never positional, so module key order is free. THE WHOLE DOCUMENT IS ALSO HASHED. planner_identity() publishes sha256(json.dumps(doc, sort_keys=True, separators=(',',':'))) as `castles_sha256` into world['castle_plans']['identity'], and terrain_history compares that identity before advancing an age: a mismatch refuses the advance. sort_keys=True means object key order and whitespace do not move the digest, but any value or list order does. The expected digest is recorded in `castles_sha256` below; a port that cannot reproduce it cannot reopen a saved world. planner_identity() also publishes `castle_buildings_sha256`, a SEPARATE digest over section('structure_blocks')['castle'] from the building registry, which is not this file; the two registries join by `structure_id`. This registry deliberately does NOT participate in city or hamlet planner identity.",
